 "literal": "Folly that-which-is-called one-thing what if-asked, harm having-taken profit to-go letting.",
 "translation": "If asked what the one thing called folly is, it is grasping what causes harm and letting go of what brings profit.",
 "modern": "பேதைமை என்று சொல்லப்படுவது என்னவென்றால், தனக்குத் துன்பம் தருவதைப் பற்றிக்கொண்டு, நன்மை தருவதைக் கைவிடுவதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet defines the core nature of folly or ignorance. The poet asks what the single defining trait of folly is, and answers by contrasting 'ētam' (harm) with 'ūtiyam' (profit or benefit). A foolish person actively grasps and holds onto things that cause them harm, while carelessly letting go of things that would bring them true benefit. Thus, folly is shown not merely as a lack of knowledge, but as an actively destructive pattern of choices.",
